<h1>TEventTriggeredCallback</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TEventTriggeredCallback" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TEventTriggeredCallback</tt> triggers a new callback request when a particular <tt>EventName</tt>
on a control with ID given by <tt>ControlID</tt> is raised.
The default action of the event on the client-side can be prevented when
<tt>PreventDefaultAction</tt> is set to true.
</p>


<h2>Properties</h2>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>ControlID</tt></td><td>The id of the control or element to watch. It is an id path relative to the trigger's own naming container.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>EventName</tt></td><td>The client-side event to watch, written without the <tt>on</tt> prefix, such as <tt>click</tt> or <tt>change</tt>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>PreventDefaultAction</tt></td><td>Whether the browser's own response to the event is suppressed. Defaults to false. Set it to true on a link or a submit button so the page does not navigate while the callback runs.</td></tr>
</table>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;div id="hotspot"&gt;Click anywhere here&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;com:TEventTriggeredCallback
    ControlID="hotspot"
    EventName="click"
    PreventDefaultAction="true"
    OnCallback="hotspotClicked" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
The trigger renders nothing of its own. It attaches to an element that already exists,
which is how plain markup, not only PRADO controls, gains a server-side handler.
</p>

